CPython 3.11 (Python's standard library)
https://github.com/python/cpython
Licence: PSF-2.0. CPython 3.11's LICENSE: "Python software and
documentation are licensed under the Python Software Foundation License
Version 2."

These files translate code of Python 3.11's standard library to Rust, each
naming it in its header:
- crates/unityrs-core/src/detect.rs: Lib/gzip.py's _read_exact,
  _read_gzip_header, _GzipReader.read and _GzipReader._read_eof, how gzip
  detection reads each member's header, inflates it and checks its trailer;
- crates/unityrs-core/src/pystr.rs: Lib/ntpath.py's splitdrive and split
  (for basename), and Lib/genericpath.py's _splitext (for splitext);
- crates/unityrs-dump/src/select/glob.rs: Lib/fnmatch.py's translate, and the
  character set of Lib/re/_parser.py's _parse;
- crates/unityrs-dump/src/select/index.rs: Modules/_json.c's
  scan_once_unicode, _match_number_unicode, scanstring_unicode,
  _parse_array_unicode and _parse_object_unicode;
- crates/unityrs-cli/src/args/mod.rs: Lib/argparse.py's
  ArgumentParser._parse_known_args (with its consume_optional),
  _parse_optional, _get_option_tuples and _negative_number_matcher.

The changes made to them (the agreement's clause 3 asks for a summary): each
is rewritten in Rust and keeps only what unityrs uses of it. The path rules
work on bytes, the glob on code points and the JSON reader on the index's
UTF-8 text, where Python works on str objects. A failure is an error value
where Python raises. The gzip reader keeps only the first 20 bytes it
inflates and the length of the whole, and stops at unityrs's inflate bound
and the open's work. The glob is matched by a wildcard matcher where Python
compiles a regular expression. The JSON reader builds the values an index
holds, as Python's json.loads does. The argument parser parses the dump's
own options, with unityrs's extensions.

